WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:02.460
If you can't judge the content,

2
00:00:02.564 --> 00:00:05.757
two questions do the most work: what would make this fail?

3
00:00:06.502 --> 00:00:07.477
And: how do you know?

4
00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.034
The first finds risks.

5
00:00:10.741 --> 00:00:12.483
The second checks the claims.

6
00:00:13.492 --> 00:00:16.940
Together they test a plan without understanding every detail.

7
00:00:18.253 --> 00:00:21.922
A premortem: the team imagines the project has already failed.

8
00:00:22.696 --> 00:00:26.224
Each person writes down why, before anyone speaks.

9
00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:31.970
Gary Klein described it in Harvard Business Review, in 2007.

10
00:00:32.981 --> 00:00:36.870
He cites 1989 research: by his account,

11
00:00:36.987 --> 00:00:41.573
imagined hindsight makes people 30 percent better at the right reasons.

12
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:46.762
The study counted about 30 percent more reasons, not their quality,

13
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.945
but more candidates is what you want.

14
00:00:50.045 --> 00:00:53.447
Writing first stops the senior voice from setting the tone.

15
00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:57.504
Watch for a single point of failure: one part,

16
00:00:57.655 --> 00:01:01.045
person or supplier that stops everything if it fails.

17
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:05.855
One server with no backup, one programmer who knows the code,

18
00:01:06.029 --> 00:01:08.235
one cable feeding the whole factory.

19
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.628
For each serious risk,

20
00:01:10.791 --> 00:01:15.272
ask for the mitigation: what makes it less likely or less bad, and who owns it?

21
00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:19.913
A risk without a mitigation and an owner is just a worry.

22
00:01:21.178 --> 00:01:25.880
The second question asks for evidence: something you can see, test or check.

23
00:01:26.616 --> 00:01:27.533
Not an opinion.

24
00:01:28.522 --> 00:01:30.589
From weak to strong: I think so.

25
00:01:31.309 --> 00:01:32.342
It worked before.

26
00:01:33.112 --> 00:01:34.238
We tested it here.

27
00:01:34.942 --> 00:01:36.219
Here is the test report.

28
00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:38.977
Ask calmly, about the claim,

29
00:01:39.128 --> 00:01:42.495
not the person: how do we know the network can carry the data?

30
00:01:43.265 --> 00:01:44.310
Not: are you sure?

31
00:01:45.339 --> 00:01:48.241
The typical failure: accepting the first answer.

32
00:01:48.958 --> 00:01:52.836
That's the comfortable one; the useful one comes after a pause.

33
00:01:53.879 --> 00:01:58.790
That is Silence and Inquiry from MORE PIES: stay quiet, then go deeper.

34
00:01:59.538 --> 00:02:01.698
If that happened, what would we see first?

35
00:02:02.763 --> 00:02:06.931
A rough rule many project leaders use: no risk that surprises you?

36
00:02:07.696 --> 00:02:09.751
Then people haven't been honest yet.

37
00:02:11.107 --> 00:02:16.192
Two months before an MES go-live: a 30 minute premortem with eight people.

38
00:02:17.206 --> 00:02:20.062
Step one: it's September, the go-live failed.

39
00:02:20.858 --> 00:02:23.737
Write down why, alone, for 5 minutes.

40
00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.412
Step two: read them out.

41
00:02:27.164 --> 00:02:29.916
The barcode scanners on line 2 didn't connect.

42
00:02:30.649 --> 00:02:32.391
The night shift wasn't trained.

43
00:02:33.137 --> 00:02:35.389
Only Ahmed knows the interface.

44
00:02:36.449 --> 00:02:39.874
Step three: group them and pick the three most serious.

45
00:02:41.013 --> 00:02:43.103
Step four: how do we know it won't happen?

46
00:02:43.834 --> 00:02:47.084
The scanners were tested in the office, not on the line.

47
00:02:47.776 --> 00:02:48.844
Weak evidence.

48
00:02:49.858 --> 00:02:54.735
Step five: a scanner test on line 2 next week, with the IT lead as owner.

49
00:02:55.754 --> 00:03:00.003
And Ahmed, the single point of failure, gets a second person trained.

50
00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:05.534
Jargon is the specialist language of a field: precise for insiders,

51
00:03:05.731 --> 00:03:07.240
opaque for everyone else.

52
00:03:08.266 --> 00:03:09.868
Not a problem in itself.

53
00:03:10.585 --> 00:03:15.299
It is when decisions get made in it that nobody outside the field understands.

54
00:03:16.342 --> 00:03:19.953
Your job: translate back into goal, cost, time and risk,

55
00:03:20.057 --> 00:03:22.089
so the sponsor and the floor can follow.

56
00:03:23.476 --> 00:03:28.143
MORE PIES is a checklist for active listening: to understand, not to reply.

57
00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:29.961
Eight letters.

58
00:03:30.718 --> 00:03:33.272
Two matter most here: the P and the I.

59
00:03:34.283 --> 00:03:37.255
The P: paraphrasing, in your own words.

60
00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:40.587
So what you're saying is, then plain words.

61
00:03:41.314 --> 00:03:42.440
Have I got that right?

62
00:03:43.455 --> 00:03:47.158
Experts usually correct you, and the correction is where you learn.

63
00:03:48.198 --> 00:03:51.891
The I: inquiry, one level deeper, to the consequence.

64
00:03:52.604 --> 00:03:54.160
What does that mean for the date?

65
00:03:54.908 --> 00:03:55.581
For the cost?

66
00:03:56.303 --> 00:03:56.976
For the risk?

67
00:03:57.983 --> 00:04:01.919
Silence helps too: after the paraphrase, wait for the correction.

68
00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:05.053
Teach-back comes from health care:

69
00:04:05.216 --> 00:04:10.150
the doctor asks the patient to explain the instructions back in their own words.

70
00:04:11.255 --> 00:04:14.959
Do it the other way round: tell his point back as to the sponsor,

71
00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:16.329
and let him fix it.

72
00:04:17.415 --> 00:04:21.408
Then you have a sentence for the steering meeting, without misquoting anyone.

73
00:04:22.403 --> 00:04:26.188
Typical failures: translating so loosely the meaning changes.

74
00:04:26.878 --> 00:04:28.248
So it's basically fine?

75
00:04:29.274 --> 00:04:32.316
Or letting the expert present to the board in jargon.

76
00:04:33.059 --> 00:04:34.290
Or pretending to follow.

77
00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.873
An expert about to present?

78
00:04:37.598 --> 00:04:42.532
First agree with him the one sentence the sponsor must remember, in plain words.

79
00:04:43.987 --> 00:04:46.019
An MES design meeting.

80
00:04:46.705 --> 00:04:49.921
The specialist: the ERP interface is batch,

81
00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:53.427
not real-time, so up to 15 minutes latency.

82
00:04:54.412 --> 00:04:57.036
And no idempotency on retries.

83
00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:02.856
You paraphrase the first part: orders reach the floor up to 15 minutes late.

84
00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:04.284
He nods.

85
00:05:05.294 --> 00:05:09.636
The second part, you ask honestly: what does that mean in plain words,

86
00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.308
and what's the consequence?

87
00:05:12.328 --> 00:05:15.358
Answer: a message sent twice can double an order.

88
00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.774
You inquire: what would that cost, and how often?

89
00:05:19.877 --> 00:05:24.556
Then teach-back, as to the sponsor: orders arrive up to 15 minutes late,

90
00:05:24.753 --> 00:05:26.576
and doubled if the link hiccups.

91
00:05:27.257 --> 00:05:28.836
So, a check for doubles.

92
00:05:29.575 --> 00:05:30.388
Is that fair?

93
00:05:31.388 --> 00:05:32.770
He adjusts one word.

94
00:05:33.509 --> 00:05:35.843
That sentence goes to the steering meeting.
